# SECTION B — Cost & KPI Measurement

## LEAF B1 — Measurement Parameters

### McKinsey five-layer AI measurement framework (origin: McKinsey QuantumBlack)
A pyramid linking technical signals up to enterprise value, with governance gates between layers. Layers progress from technical performance and adoption signals → operational improvements → financial benefits/ROI → durable enterprise value. McKinsey's four canonical business-value placements: **lower cost to serve, revenue uplift, margin expansion, and total cost of ownership** (model usage + licensing). Its overarching finding: workflow redesign has "the biggest effect on an organization's ability to see EBIT impact from gen AI." Per McKinsey's *The state of AI* (March 2025, QuantumBlack), only **6%** of organizations qualify as "AI high performers" with ≥5% EBIT impact, only **39%** report enterprise-level EBIT impact, and **78%** use AI in at least one business function (up from 72% in early 2024). The "10-20-70 principle": 70% of effort to people/process/culture, 20% to data/tech, 10% to algorithms.

### SPACE framework (origin: Forsgren, Storey, Maddila, Zimmermann, Houck, Butler — Microsoft Research/GitHub/Univ. Victoria; ACM Queue, 2021)
Five dimensions of (developer) productivity, explicitly multidimensional because "no single metric can capture productivity": **S**atisfaction & well-being, **P**erformance (outcomes), **A**ctivity (volume of actions), **C**ommunication & collaboration, **E**fficiency & flow. Warns against using Activity (commits, PRs) as a standalone proxy.

### DevEx framework (origin: Noda, Storey, Forsgren et al.)
Refines SPACE into three core developer-experience drivers: **feedback loops, cognitive load, and flow state**, with cognitive-load reduction identified as the highest-leverage intervention. Complementary to **DORA** delivery metrics.

### Microsoft Work Trend Index + Copilot measurement (origin: Microsoft & LinkedIn)
The 2024 Work Trend Index Annual Report ("AI at Work Is Here. Now Comes the Hard Part," May 8 2024) surveyed 31,000 workers across 31 markets via Edelman Data & Intelligence. Headline metrics: 75% of knowledge workers use generative AI at work; 78% of AI users are "bringing their own AI" (BYOAI); users report time savings (90%), focus (85%), creativity (84%), enjoyment (83%); 60% of leaders say their company lacks an implementation plan and 59% worry about quantifying productivity gains. Microsoft operationalizes measurement via the **Copilot Dashboard / Copilot Analytics** (in Viva / Copilot Control System) tracking adoption, usage and self-reported time saved. The **Forrester Total Economic Impact (TEI)** study commissioned by Microsoft (2024, composite of 367 decision-makers) projected ~$18.8M productivity benefits — *a vendor-commissioned, composite, partly self-reported projection that should be treated cautiously.*

### GitHub Copilot productivity research (origin: GitHub; Peng, Kalliamvakou, Cihon & Demirer, 2022/2023)
Per Peng, Kalliamvakou, Cihon & Demirer, *The Impact of AI on Developer Productivity: Evidence from GitHub Copilot* (arXiv:2302.06590, 2023): "the treated group completed the task 55.8% faster (95% confidence interval: 21-89%)" — 71.17 minutes (treated) vs 160.89 minutes (control), p=0.0017, on a JavaScript HTTP-server implementation task. A GitHub survey of 2,000+ developers found 88% felt more productive. *Caveat: the 55.8% figure reflects a single narrow coding task, not real-world production output, and is routinely over-generalized.*

### The "AI value equation" (benefit drivers minus cost drivers)
Conceptually: **Value = Σ(benefit drivers) − Σ(cost drivers)**, where benefit drivers = time saved (converted to $), throughput/volume gains (margin), error/rework reduction, revenue uplift; and cost drivers = license fees, implementation, compute/usage, change management, and **risk/non-compliance costs**. A representative CFO formulation uses metrics such as time-to-output-reduction (TTOR), error/rework rate, and cost-per-output, net of fully-loaded AI costs, noting well-scoped programs typically reach positive ROI at month 8–12.

### Metric taxonomy for this leaf
- **Adoption:** activation rate, retention, frequency, depth/breadth of use, license utilization.
- **Productivity:** time saved, throughput, cycle time, quality/error rate.
- **Business value / P&L:** cost-to-serve, revenue uplift, margin, TCO.
- **Leading vs lagging:** adoption/usage are leading; EBIT impact is lagging.

## LEAF B2 — Tracking Process & Ownership

- **Metric ownership / RACI:** assign one Accountable owner per metric; a value/measurement office or **AI Center of Excellence (CoE)** consolidates standards, shares learnings, and accelerates deployment (McKinsey explicitly recommends building CoEs).
- **Measurement cadence & instrumentation:** McKinsey's pyramid shifts measurement from manual pilot-phase review to telemetry "built directly into the AI tool" at MVP/scale. Combine **system telemetry** (usage logs, license utilization) with **survey instruments** (self-reported time saved, satisfaction — as in SPACE's Satisfaction dimension and the Work Trend Index).
- **Baselining & counterfactuals:** lock in attribution method *before* the pilot — A/B testing, staggered/phased rollout, or control groups — to isolate AI's effect.
- **FinOps-style cost tracking:** treat model/usage costs like cloud spend (per-user, per-app, per-model token tracking, quotas, alerting) — directly applicable to per-seat Copilot/ChatGPT/Claude licensing plus consumption.
- **Dashboards/scorecards:** the Copilot Dashboard is the vendor-native example; a balanced scorecard spanning adoption/productivity/value/risk is the general pattern.

## LEAF B3 — Pilot Scaling Criteria

**The core finding:** most pilots fail to scale for organizational, not technical, reasons. Per BCG's *Where's the Value in AI?* (October 24, 2024; survey of 1,000 CxOs/senior executives across 59 countries), "only 26 per cent of the companies have developed the necessary set of capabilities to move beyond proofs of concept and generate tangible value," and just 4% generate significant value across functions. BCG's **"10-20-70" rule** from the same report frames AI transformation as "10% algorithms, 20% data and technology, and 70% people, processes, and cultural change." Gartner predicts 60% of AI projects abandoned through 2026 absent AI-ready data, and forecast 30% of GenAI projects abandoned after POC by end of 2025. Common root causes: data quality/infrastructure, absent governance, neglected change management, and misaligned (technical-only) metrics.

### Stage-gate scaling criteria (the pilot-to-scale gate)
Synthesizing McKinsey's pyramid and consultancy guidance, a pilot graduates only when, at the gate:
- **Adoption** extends beyond early enthusiasts (sustained, not novelty-driven);
- **Operational improvements** are statistically significant;
- **Financial benefits** at least offset total cost of ownership;
- **Technical performance** holds under higher load with monitoring/retraining functioning;
- **Risk/compliance** requirements are met (risk clearance);
- **Change-management readiness** exists (process redesign, training, ownership).
McKinsey: "If ROI is not evident at this stage, it is a signal to refine or stop before full-scale investment."

### "Scale / fix / kill" and proof-of-value (POV) exit criteria
Two-stage measurement: **technical metrics in the pilot** (accuracy, exception rate, throughput) → **business KPIs in production** (cycle-time, cost-per-transaction, headcount reallocation). Deloitte finds business-aligned-metric organizations achieve payback in under two years vs two-to-four for technically-measured ones. The decision at the gate is explicitly trinary: scale (evidence supports BAU embedding), fix (refine and re-gate), or kill (sunk-cost discipline).

