## LEAF C2 — Ongoing Education, Agentic AI ("Cowork") & Connectors

### Governing agentic AI (autonomous multi-step work)
- **NIST AI 600-1 (Generative AI Profile, July 2024)** — cross-sectoral companion to the AI RMF identifying **12 GenAI risk categories** (CBRN information; confabulation; dangerous/violent/hateful content; data privacy; environmental impacts; harmful bias & homogenization; human-AI configuration; information integrity; information security; intellectual property; obscene/degrading content; value-chain & component integration) with 200+ suggested actions organized around governance, content provenance, pre-deployment testing, and incident disclosure. A **proposed NIST AI RMF Agentic Profile** (Cloud Security Alliance Labs) extends RMF for agent autonomy, tool-use risk, runtime governance, and delegation-chain accountability.
- **OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025)** — names **LLM06: Excessive Agency** (excessive functionality/permissions/autonomy) with mitigations: least-privilege design, scoped tokens, human-in-the-loop for state-changing actions, capability minimization (read-only where sufficient), action validation. Also relevant: LLM01 Prompt Injection, LLM02 Sensitive Information Disclosure, LLM05 Improper Output Handling, LLM10 Unbounded Consumption. The **OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications (Dec 2025)** adds ASI03 Identity & Privilege Abuse and the "principle of least agency."
- **Vendor frameworks** converge on: **human-in-the-loop checkpoints** (Anthropic Claude Code read-only by default; "Plan Mode" reviews the whole execution plan upfront; OpenAI "constraining the action space" and "interruptibility"; Salesforce escalation rules); **action scoping** (Salesforce Topics/Actions/Instructions; Microsoft Copilot Studio "predefined actions, approved connectors, and controlled capabilities rather than arbitrary code execution"); **least-privilege** (Microsoft "no new privileges" — agents respect existing M365 permissions); and **audit trails** (Salesforce generative-AI audit trail in Data 360; Microsoft Purview DSPM for AI). OpenAI's seven practices (Dec 2023): evaluate suitability, constrain action space, set default behaviors, legibility of agent activity, automatic monitoring, attributability, interruptibility/maintaining control. Cloud Security Alliance's **MAESTRO** (Feb 2025) is a seven-layer agentic threat-modeling framework; its **AI Controls Matrix (AICM)** spans 243 controls across 18 domains.

### Governing connectors / plug-ins / third-party integrations
- **Principle:** least-privilege + permission inheritance. Microsoft 365 Copilot agents respect existing permissions ("no new privileges"); ChatGPT Enterprise connectors/apps are disabled by default for Enterprise with per-user OAuth, RBAC and action control; Claude MCP lets admins set allowed connectors with one-time/permanent grants and an Anthropic-reviewed MCP directory.
- **Review/approval:** treat each connector as a third-party integration requiring security review; OWASP and OpenAI warn custom/unverified connectors should only be added if the underlying app is trusted ("custom apps are not verified by OpenAI… only add custom apps to your workspace if you know and trust the underlying application"). Cloud Security Alliance flags the **non-human identity governance vacuum** (NHIs outnumber human identities ~90:1) — argue for short-lived tokens, JIT provisioning, and zero-trust for agent/connector identities. The Five Eyes agencies' *Careful Adoption of Agentic AI Services* (May 1, 2026) requires each agent to carry "a verified, cryptographically anchored identity with short-lived credentials."
- **Allow vs disallow:** allow connectors that inherit user permissions, have scoped OAuth, and are vendor-reviewed; disallow/escalate write-capable, broadly-scoped, or unverified connectors without human approval gates.

### Ongoing user education / continuous enablement
- McKinsey's value research identifies **role-based capability training** as a top management practice distinguishing high performers, alongside leaders role-modeling use, embedding gen AI into processes, and feedback mechanisms. The general model: continuous enablement (not one-time training), capability-update communication as features evolve, and role-based curricula. Salesforce's AI-literacy program and Trailhead are a vendor exemplar.

## LEAF C3 — Breach Escalation & Resolution

### NIST SP 800-61 (Computer Security Incident Handling Guide) adapted to AI
- **Rev. 2 (2012)** four-phase lifecycle: **Preparation; Detection & Analysis; Containment, Eradication & Recovery; Post-Incident Activity** (lessons learned). The cycle is non-linear with feedback loops.
- **Rev. 3 (April 2025)** restructures around **NIST CSF 2.0** functions (Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover), aligning incident response with enterprise risk management and emphasizing continuous improvement. For AI misuse/appropriate-use violations, adapt: severity/escalation tiers, triage (true incident vs false positive), defined roles (incident manager, forensic analyst, IT, legal), containment matched to the incident type, remediation workflows, and a formal lessons-learned loop feeding back into preparation.

### NIST AI RMF "MANAGE" function
Allocates risk-treatment resources to mapped/measured risks; "risk treatment comprises plans to respond to, recover from, and communicate about incidents or events," with "mechanisms for continual improvement" and "processes for assessing emergent risks." This is the AI-native counterpart to incident response.

### AI Incident Database (origin: Responsible AI Collaborative)
A public repository of AI incidents/harms supporting the detection/learning phases — used to benchmark severity, learn from analogous incidents, and inform preparation.

### Synthesis — an AI incident-response playbook
Combine SP 800-61r3 structure (CSF 2.0 functions) + AI RMF MANAGE + NIST AI 600-1's **incident disclosure** consideration: (1) **Preparation** — AI AUP, escalation tiers, defined owners, tabletop exercises; (2) **Detection & triage** — telemetry + user reporting, classify by severity (e.g., data leakage vs policy violation vs harmful output); (3) **Containment/eradication/recovery** — scope-match response (e.g., revoke connector, disable agent action), preserve evidence; (4) **Post-incident** — lessons learned, update playbooks/policy, continuous-improvement loop. Severity tiers and clear decision rights (RACI/RAPID) determine who handles which class of AI policy violation.

